{
 "text": "Government websites in four countries just switched themselves off. Nobody hacked them. They were fixing a security bug in PHP, the programming language behind WordPress and about 7 out of 10 websites. Fixing a bug like that takes two steps. First, you find it. Then, you fix it everywhere. You'd think finding it is the hard part, right? And for 14 years, nobody did. Then, in August, two security teams using AI tools found it and quietly told the people who make PHP so a fix could be ready before anyone else knew. Except, the details of the bug leaked online hours before the fix came out. Cloudflare, which protects a big chunk of the web, says it saw tens of thousands of break-in attempts in 45 minutes, and millions by evening. Nobody knows yet how many got in. So now comes step two, the slow one. Someone has to install that fix on every site, and there are about 22 million copies of PHP out there that could be hit. If a site can't update fast enough, the safest thing is to switch it off until it can. That's what those government sites did. And one in three sites on PHP runs a version so old that the people who make PHP don't fix it anymore. For those, it's switch off or hope the hosting company sorts it out.",
